this morning we're looking at how  increased screen time during the  pandemic is affecting our vision  since this pandemic first hit the  average screen time per person has  spiked  more than 13 hours a day that's a lot  according to eyesafe nielsen  estimates 60 percent of people in one  recent survey say they were worried  about how this will affect their eyes  doctors also say  they're seeing an uptick in vision  issues like dry eye yep i got that  dr christopher starr he was an  ophthalmologist at  weill cornell medical in medicine rather  in new york  joins us with some solutions and whether  he thinks products like blue light  glasses can help  good morning to you doctor good to see  you  hi gail how are you i'm all right with  my dry eye sitting up here but a lot of  people are looking at their screens more  than ever  can you explain why it's such a bad why  it's bad for your eyesight to begin with  yeah it's it's multifactorial we call it  the computer  vision syndrome and it it it combines  both eye strain from just staring at the  computers which are right in front of  you  for all those hours as you said 13 hours  or more  but it also when we're on the computers  when we're staring and fatiguing our  eyes we're also staring and not blinking  as much so the blink rate which is  normally about 16 to 18 times a sec  a minute decreases by about 50 percent  to maybe eight blinks a minute and so  what happens there is the tears  evaporate  and we get these dry spots and the eyes  get drier and drier and redder and more  irritated and gritty as the day goes on  and that leads to fluctuations in vision  and blurred vision and then of course  the eye strain can cause headaches and  all this  so the computer vision syndrome is a  real problem especially  now in the post covid era yeah i never  thought about the lack of blinking what  are the signs of digital eye strain  people listening to you right now are  thinking do i have that  but probably most of us do have it yeah  it's uh you know the the eye strain  component  is is often headaches as the day goes on  frontal headaches pain around the eyes  um the pain behind the eyes and then the  dry eye portion of it is usually  grittiness foreign body sensation the  vision can fluctuate a lot and sometimes  you blink a little bit to clear the  vision and then it gets blurry again  redness and sometimes excessive tearing  burning  all those symptoms are related to this  dryness and the computer  vision syndrome that we're seeing but it  can it can be really hard to take a  break from this  and my daughter's working for home she's  tried blue light glasses  uh with with some help do they generally  work i don't know how much they've done  for her  you know i mean taking breaks is  important and i think that's  the real key here not so much glasses or  filters over your screens taking the  breaks  taking breaks we recommend every 20  minutes or so take a break  look away look into the distance at an  object that's 20 feet away or further  looking at a window is actually perfect  for 20 seconds or more that's what we  used to call the 20 20 20  rule and then i usually add an extra 20  to that the fourth 20 would be during  those breaks  either close your eyes in the you know  sort of zoom era  we can actually close our eyes and not  be judged by our ours  our superiors our bosses you can just  close those eyes on what you're doing  there by closing the eyes  for 20 seconds is to just re  re-lubricate the eyes and get those  tears across the ocular surface  so 20 20 20 20 is very important  the blue light question blue light is  kind of  stimulating and so we usually recommend  blocking blue light  at night when you're trying to go to bed  you want melatonin blue light will block  the melatonin and so  you know ideally no devices in the  bedroom at all but if you're going to  have to look at your phone or your  computer  you should dim the blue lights you don't  need the special glasses  i mean certainly you can use them if you  have them but most of the computers  these days will have  something called a night mode or night  shift mode where you can actually turn  off the blue pixels you know  and so i have mine set to you know nine  o'clock at night  the screen turns yellow and all those  blue pixels disappear  i gotta try that one i don't like that  feature  dr christopher star some very  interesting tips there thank you  we appreciate it
 

